, and a strong Democratic midterm environment all point to a modest Brooks lead, and prediction markets price him near 80% to flip the seat. Mackenzie's fundraising advantage and narrow 2024 win keep this within reach, but the weight of evidence favors a Democratic gain of about four points.", "watch": "A shift in the national environment or a strong Mackenzie debate and ad performance could tighten this back toward a true tossup.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Incumbent Ryan Mackenzie (R) is running."]}, "overview": "First-term Republican Ryan Mackenzie, who won this Lehigh Valley seat by the narrowest margin in Pennsylvania two years ago, faces Democrat Bob Brooks in a rematch-style contest that both parties view as pivotal for House control. Mackenzie has out-raised Brooks and holds a large cash advantage, but outside spending has flowed heavily toward Brooks.\n\nRecent polling shows Brooks ahead, with a September Franklin & Marshall survey putting him up five among registered voters amid low Trump approval. Most expert raters call the race a tossup, though several Democratic-leaning forecasters rate it a Democratic flip, and prediction markets price Brooks near 80% to win. The race sits as a modest but genuine Democratic edge heading into November.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.7234, "margin": 3.52}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.7148, "margin": 3.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.7673, "margin": 4.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.7925, "margin": 4.5}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.7174, "margin": 4.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.7271, "margin": 4.2}]}